course: biology
lesson: 109
title: Behind the scenes: a day with the keepers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Long before the gates open, at about 7 in the morning, the keepers walk round the zoo and the first job is simple: look at every animal and count them, to be sure all are there and all are well.
explain: Then comes the real work: shovelling dung, scrubbing pools, cutting food, writing notes, and every day of the year, Christmas included, because animals need care whatever the date.
explain: A keeper knows every meerkat in a group by its face, marks and manners, the way your teacher knows every child in your class, and that is how she spots the one who is ill or the one who is boss.
explain: In Madagascar the zoo animals slip out at night and ride the subway to the city; real escapes are rare, and zoos practise for them like a school fire drill, with teams, radios and nets.
fact: A keeper's day starts before the zoo opens with checking and counting every animal, then cleaning, feeding and writing notes, and keepers work in shifts because animals need care every day, weekends and Christmas too.
fact: Keepers write down what each animal ate, how it acted and anything unusual, because small changes over days show illness early, and many zoos share records on a system called ZIMS.
fact: Cleaning is a big part of the job, and dung is checked, because its look and amount tell keepers about an animal's digestion and health; some zoos sell «zoo poo» compost from plant-eaters to gardeners.
fact: A keeper never goes into the den of a dangerous animal like a tiger while it is there; the animal is first moved through slide doors, with two-step locks, checks, radios and keepers often working in pairs.
fact: Keepers have «hands-off» rules for dangerous animals and «hands-on» care for gentle ones.
fact: Keepers usually study animal care or biology and start as volunteers or trainees, and many specialise in birds, reptiles or big mammals; the job needs knowledge, safety and lots of patience.
fact: Keepers tell animals apart by marks, scars, shape and behaviour, which helps them see who is ill, who is boss and who needs extra care, and some animals also have tiny microchips under the skin, like pet dogs.
fact: Many animals have quiet off-show areas where they can rest and hide; good zoos let animals choose whether to be seen, which is why you sometimes see an empty enclosure.
fact: Reptile and amphibian rooms have heaters, lights and mist machines controlled by computers to copy each animal's home, and reptiles need special UV lamps to make vitamin D for strong bones.
fact: Zoos practise escape drills with teams, radios and nets, a bit like fire drills at school; real escapes are rare, and a famous escaper was Ken Allen, an orangutan at San Diego Zoo in the 1980s.
fact: Keepers build trust with animals over months and years through calm, regular routines, which makes health checks easier, and many keepers find it hard to say goodbye when an animal moves to another zoo.
fact: Some zoos have night staff to check animals that are ill or about to give birth, because births often happen at night, and cameras let keepers watch without disturbing the animals.
exercise: What is often a keeper's very first job in the morning? | Checking every animal | Opening the gates for visitors | Selling tickets at the entrance | Feeding the goldfish in the café pond
exercise: Why do keepers work in shifts, even at Christmas? | Animals need care every single day | Keepers like Christmas work | Zoos are busiest on Christmas Day | Animals only eat on holidays
exercise: Why do keepers write notes about every animal each day? | To spot illness early | To make a book for visitors to buy | Because the law says they must write a poem each day | To remember which animals they like best
exercise: What is ZIMS? | A shared record system that lets zoos see an animal's history | A brand of dry animal food | A lock on the tiger's door | The name of a famous keeper
exercise: Why do keepers look carefully at dung? | It tells them about an animal's digestion and health | They are looking for lost keys | They only want to see what colour it is today, nothing more | It is the only way to count the animals
exercise: What do some zoos make from plant-eaters' dung? | Compost for gardeners | Bricks for new enclosures | Food for the hungry lions | Fuel for the zoo train
exercise: How does a keeper clean the tiger's den safely? | The tiger is moved out through slide doors first | The keeper waits till the tiger sleeps | The keeper carries a big stick | The keeper goes in quietly at night
exercise: What is a «hands-off» animal? | A dangerous one that keepers do not touch | An animal with no hands | A shy animal that hides all day | An animal that only eats from bowls, never from hands
exercise: How do people usually become zoo keepers? | Study animal care or biology and start as volunteers or trainees | Win a competition at the zoo | Be born into a family of keepers, as the job is passed down to children | Buy the job from the zoo
exercise: How does a keeper tell meerkats apart? | By their marks and habits | By the coloured hats they wear | She cannot, as they all look exactly the same | By asking each one its name
exercise: Why do zoos fit some animals with microchips? | To identify them for sure, like pet dogs | To track them by satellite | To give them medicine slowly | To let them open doors
exercise: Why are there quiet off-show rooms behind the scenes? | Animals feel safer when they can get away from crowds | To store food and tools only | To hide sick animals from inspectors | To keep the animals the zoo does not like out of sight
exercise: Why do reptiles need special UV lamps? | To make vitamin D and keep their bones strong | To stop their skin turning blue | To help them see in the dark | To stop them shedding their skin
exercise: Why do zoos practise escape drills? | So everyone knows exactly what to do in an emergency | Because animals escape every week | To give the animals exercise | To scare the animals into behaving well for the visitors
exercise: Who was Ken Allen? | An orangutan who escaped several times at San Diego Zoo | A keeper who caught a runaway lion | A giraffe that could open doors | A famous zoo vet in the 1980s
exercise: Why do keepers keep the same calm routine every day? | It helps animals trust them, which makes health checks easier | Animals cannot tell the time otherwise and would miss their meals | It is quicker to clean that way | So visitors know when to come
exercise: Why do some zoos have keepers on duty at night? | Births often happen at night and sick animals need checks | To stop the animals oversleeping | To feed the animals a midnight snack | To lock the gift shop
test: The keeper starts at 7 and the zoo opens at 10. How many hours of work before visitors come? | 3 | 2 | 4 | 17
test: «zoo keepers just feed the cute ones and chill, easy job» What is true? | They clean, feed, check health and write notes every day | Yes, feeding is the only job | Yes, the animals clean their own homes, so keepers just relax | No, keepers only sell tickets
test: The keeper counts 12 meerkats in the morning. How many ears and tails is that altogether? | 36 | 24 | 12 | 48
test: In Madagascar the animals walk out of the zoo at night. What is true of real zoos? | Escapes are rare, and zoos practise drills for them | Animals escape every night and quietly come back by morning | Zoos leave the gates open at night | Real zoos have no locks at all
test: What would happen if a keeper forgot to check the slide door before going into the tiger's den? | The keeper could be badly hurt, which is why there are two-step checks | Nothing, because zoo tigers are as tame as cats and never attack keepers | The tiger would run away scared | The tiger would just want to play
test: «went to see the red pandas and there was nothing there, rubbish zoo» Why might that happen? | Good zoos let animals choose to hide off-show | The zoo sold all the red pandas | Red pandas only come out for keepers | The animals were all away at the vet for the whole day
test: «keeper's got a giraffe licking her hand, I wanna try» What is right? | Keepers built that trust over months; visitors must stay back | Fine, any giraffe likes any person | Fine, if you bring some leaves | Fine, giraffes are such gentle animals that they cannot hurt people
test: A lizard's lamp breaks over the weekend. What problem could follow? | Without UV it cannot make vitamin D, and its bones get weak | It will turn into a frog | Nothing, because lizards do not need any light at all to stay well | It will grow too fast
test: «my nan says zoo keepers are just cleaners» What is right? | Cleaning is a big part, but they also check health, feed and train | Yes, that is all they do | No, keepers never clean anything, because the zoo pays other cleaners | No, robots do the cleaning
test: A keeper notices one meerkat is eating less for three days running. Why does her diary help? | It shows a small change early, so the vet can check | It proves the meerkat is greedy | It means the food is wrong for all the meerkats in the group | Nothing, eating less is always fine
test: «why's the zoo selling poo??» What is the answer? | Plant-eaters' dung makes good garden compost | It is a joke for visitors | It is sold as tasty food for the other zoo animals | It is used to paint signs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the first duty of a zoo keeper at the commencement of the working day? | The inspection and enumeration of every animal to ascertain that all are present and well | The opening of the gates to members of the public | The preparation of the evening meal for every animal in the collection before the gates open | The sale of admission tickets
exam: Identify the principal reason for which keepers maintain written records of the conduct and diet of each animal. | Small changes observed over several days may reveal illness at an early stage | Records are required solely for publication to visitors | Records serve chiefly to establish which of the animals the keeper personally prefers | Records are kept only for animals of great value
exam: Select the response consistent with the established procedures governing entry into the den of a dangerous animal. | The animal is first removed through sliding doors, the locks being checked in two stages | The keeper enters while the animal sleeps | The keeper enters alone, armed with a stick | The keeper enters only at night, when the animal is calm and drowsy after its evening meal
exam: State the reason for which reptiles kept in captivity require ultraviolet lamps. | To enable the synthesis of vitamin D for the maintenance of sound bones | To render their skin visible in darkness | To prevent the shedding of the skin | To maintain the temperature of their water
